WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.342
In a few spots in Jakarta,
European Space Agency radar showed

00:00:04.377 --> 00:00:10.170
the ground sinking almost five
inches in a year, in 2017 and 2018.

00:00:10.495 --> 00:00:15.952
A peak at individual spots,
not a citywide average, measured at an angle:

00:00:16.277 --> 00:00:18.530
along the satellite's line of sight.

00:00:18.913 --> 00:00:22.071
The satellite circled about 430 miles up.

00:00:22.396 --> 00:00:24.265
It cannot watch the ground move.

00:00:24.509 --> 00:00:28.015
It measures the distance
to the ground, pass after pass.

00:00:28.398 --> 00:00:30.639
So here is what we are going to find out.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.903
Why is the ground falling?

00:00:33.286 --> 00:00:36.212
Why has one of the sinking
areas slowed down?

00:00:36.537 --> 00:00:39.637
And why can't the same
satellites see part of the coast?

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.490
Jakarta sits on the northwest coast
of the island of Java, in Indonesia.

00:00:44.768 --> 00:00:50.318
It is built on a river delta, crossed
by thirteen natural and artificial rivers.

00:00:50.643 --> 00:00:54.045
Almost eleven million people
live in the capital province.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:59.188
And by some estimates, as much as 40 percent
of the city is already below sea level.

00:00:59.629 --> 00:01:00.709
How does that work?

00:01:01.092 --> 00:01:06.108
The radar sends microwave pulses
down and records each echo's phase:

00:01:06.317 --> 00:01:08.802
where in the wave's cycle it arrives.

00:01:09.185 --> 00:01:11.855
On a later pass, it compares the echoes.

00:01:12.296 --> 00:01:18.125
If the distance changed, even by a small
fraction of a wavelength, the phase shifts.

00:01:18.635 --> 00:01:20.342
Now the catch.

00:01:20.853 --> 00:01:25.694
The satellite measures the change in distance
along its line of sight, at an angle.

00:01:25.938 --> 00:01:27.529
That is not the vertical drop.

00:01:27.703 --> 00:01:30.954
Other passes and GPS help
estimate the vertical part.

00:01:31.163 --> 00:01:35.098
So every number here is labeled:
line of sight or vertical,

00:01:35.273 --> 00:01:37.212
what was measured, and when.

00:01:37.653 --> 00:01:39.534
So why is Jakarta sinking?

00:01:39.859 --> 00:01:43.655
Scientists who have measured it
for decades name three suspects.

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:46.418
Groundwater pumped out
from underneath the city.

00:01:46.627 --> 00:01:50.621
The weight of buildings and roads
on soft, compressible ground.

00:01:50.946 --> 00:01:53.768
And the natural settling
of young river mud.

00:01:54.372 --> 00:01:58.097
ESA's own caption points
largely at the groundwater.

00:01:58.097 --> 00:01:59.711
Why so much pumping?

00:02:00.407 --> 00:02:02.474
One 2022 study found

00:02:02.532 --> 00:02:06.445
that piped water reached only
64 percent of the province.

00:02:06.886 --> 00:02:10.938
Its authors proposed extending
the pipes first to the north and west,

00:02:11.112 --> 00:02:13.271
as one way to cut the over-pumping.

00:02:13.875 --> 00:02:18.763
When groundwater is pumped out,
soft layers of ground can compact.

00:02:19.366 --> 00:02:24.858
Researchers separate two things: ground
that springs back a little if water returns,

00:02:24.951 --> 00:02:27.134
and ground that stays squeezed.

00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:30.721
This animation is an illustration,
not a measurement.

00:02:31.104 --> 00:02:32.450
None of this is new.

00:02:32.776 --> 00:02:35.028
Surveyors, GPS crews,

00:02:35.051 --> 00:02:39.022
and radar satellites have been
measuring Jakarta for decades.

00:02:39.533 --> 00:02:45.303
Between 1974 and 2010, their typical
numbers were three to ten centimeters

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:48.112
a year, depending
on the place and the time.

00:02:48.902 --> 00:02:54.800
A recent analysis of radar data
from 2016 to 2024 found three

00:02:54.858 --> 00:02:59.235
hotspots: one in the west,
one on the northwest coast,

00:02:59.432 --> 00:03:03.414
and one in the northeast, near
the harbor at Tanjung Priok.

00:03:03.913 --> 00:03:08.267
The fastest spot sank about 30
millimeters a year, roughly an inch,

00:03:08.325 --> 00:03:09.880
along the line of sight.

00:03:10.322 --> 00:03:13.909
But count the hotspots a different
way, and you get a different answer.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.867
A separate study, still
a preprint, combined radar and GPS

00:03:18.948 --> 00:03:24.289
from 2014 to 2025 and counted
six main subsidence bowls.

00:03:24.614 --> 00:03:29.861
In the worst one, the vertical drop
reached up to 7.7 centimeters a year,

00:03:30.036 --> 00:03:31.348
about three inches.

00:03:31.951 --> 00:03:33.844
Across the whole metro area,

00:03:34.053 --> 00:03:38.163
the slow background sinking
was about 1.1 centimeters a year.

00:03:38.488 --> 00:03:44.258
Its authors point out that it relies
on a single continuous GPS station.

00:03:44.258 --> 00:03:45.988
Look at it district by district.

00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.864
A radar study of 2019 to 2020
reported average sinking rates

00:03:50.899 --> 00:03:56.507
of 4.9 centimeters a year
in North Jakarta, 4.8 in West Jakarta,

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:02.590
3.1 in Central Jakarta, 2.8
in the South, and 2.5 in the East.

00:04:03.287 --> 00:04:07.315
The abstract does not say
if these are line of sight or vertical.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:12.668
The sinking was mostly near the coast
and river mouths, on soft river deposits.

00:04:13.109 --> 00:04:15.384
On the ground, you can see the damage.

00:04:15.825 --> 00:04:19.576
That same study reported
damaged roads at Mutiara Beach,

00:04:19.727 --> 00:04:22.513
West Cengkareng, and Pademangan.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:27.691
Other researchers list cracked buildings
and roads, changed river flow,

00:04:27.773 --> 00:04:31.221
bigger flooded areas,
and drains that stop working.

00:04:31.824 --> 00:04:33.543
Now the catch.

00:04:34.239 --> 00:04:39.429
To trust a pixel, the radar echo has to stay
consistent from one pass to the next.

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:43.144
In the 2016 to 2024 analysis,

00:04:43.388 --> 00:04:47.719
any pixel with a coherence
below 0.6 was thrown out.

00:04:48.229 --> 00:04:51.747
And that filter removes pixels
along the northern coast,

00:04:51.956 --> 00:04:54.313
the part of Jakarta closest to the sea.

00:04:54.824 --> 00:04:58.319
Scientists check the radar against GPS.

00:04:58.702 --> 00:05:04.414
At one station, the radar's line-of-sight
rate came out 1.64 millimeters a year

00:05:04.484 --> 00:05:10.196
higher than the GPS vertical rate,
with a typical error of 9.40 millimeters.

00:05:10.521 --> 00:05:13.923
And that station is not in the same
spot as the radar pixel,

00:05:14.167 --> 00:05:16.036
so even this check has limits.

00:05:16.477 --> 00:05:17.742
Now the twist.

00:05:18.067 --> 00:05:21.736
The western hotspot started
sinking fast in 2016,

00:05:21.818 --> 00:05:24.023
and then slowed down significantly.

00:05:24.407 --> 00:05:27.228
The northwest and northeast
hotspots did not.

00:05:27.553 --> 00:05:29.538
They kept sinking in a straight line.

00:05:29.979 --> 00:05:34.333
A 2026 study, which we
have only read as an abstract,

00:05:34.577 --> 00:05:39.987
also reports that some areas are slowing,
a few even show localized uplift,

00:05:40.196 --> 00:05:41.961
and the coast keeps sinking.

00:05:42.344 --> 00:05:45.977
Slowing down is not the same
as coming back up.

00:05:45.977 --> 00:05:46.917
Why does it matter?

00:05:47.498 --> 00:05:49.193
Jakarta already floods.

00:05:49.623 --> 00:05:53.419
A flood in 2007 covered
more than 70 percent of the city.

00:05:53.837 --> 00:05:58.829
On New Year's Eve 2019, a rain gauge
at Halim Perdanakusuma recorded

00:05:58.899 --> 00:06:04.170
377 millimeters of rain in a single
day, nearly fifteen inches.

00:06:05.122 --> 00:06:11.054
From 1993 to a 2018 assessment,
satellites measured global sea level

00:06:11.333 --> 00:06:14.805
rising about 3.1 millimeters
a year on average.

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:19.739
In the worst bowl of that preprint,
the vertical drop was many times faster.

00:06:20.122 --> 00:06:24.023
Along the waterfront, seawater
already tops the coastal dykes,

00:06:24.046 --> 00:06:26.020
according to a survey of residents.

00:06:26.461 --> 00:06:31.895
And a 2017 model found that a three-meter
dyke, enough for today's conditions,

00:06:32.104 --> 00:06:36.202
could lose its ability to stop
floods completely by the year 2040,

00:06:36.411 --> 00:06:38.385
if the ground keeps sinking quickly.

00:06:38.989 --> 00:06:43.726
Its conclusion: stopping the sinking
would be the most effective countermeasure.

00:06:44.004 --> 00:06:45.792
So what do satellites prove?

00:06:46.175 --> 00:06:49.507
That parts of Jakarta
are sinking, at different speeds,

00:06:49.681 --> 00:06:51.794
and that some of that sinking has slowed.

00:06:52.398 --> 00:06:54.163
What can they not prove?

00:06:54.767 --> 00:06:59.875
What happens where the radar goes blind,
and exactly what is driving each bowl.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:03.927
And the next time you see a map
of a sinking city, check the label.

00:07:04.623 --> 00:07:08.130
What was measured, when,
and in which direction.
